# P6 revision, part 2 — the UI

## Context

Asked directly whether the UI was updated to let a user configure the model. It
was not. The previous commit changed exactly one JS line — the request field name
— and I had not audited what the interface actually *tells* the user.

The picker mechanism was in fact fine: `loadLlmEndpoints()` fetches
`/mapping/wizard/llm-endpoints`, which now serves `LLMTarget.models()`, so the
dropdown populates from `ONTOBRICKS_LLM_MODELS`. Verified directly: nothing
configured → `[]`, `_MODEL` only → one entry, `_MODELS` list → trimmed entries,
Databricks endpoint names → passed through unchanged.

What was wrong was the copy. Every label still told the user this had to be a
Databricks endpoint, which is no longer true — and label drift is invisible to
every other test: the picker works, the routes pass, and the only thing broken is
what the user is told.

## Changes

1. **`partials/domain/_domain_information.html`** — "LLM Endpoint" → **"Model"**.
   The help text said *"Select the Databricks Model Serving endpoint"*; the alert
   said *"The endpoint must be a Databricks Model Serving endpoint accessible from
   this workspace"*. Both replaced with the actual contract, naming
   `ONTOBRICKS_LLM_BASE_URL` / `_API_KEY` / `_MODELS` so an operator seeing an
   empty picker knows what to set.

2. **`global/js/utils.js`** (new-domain dialog) — same relabel; the footnote
   *"Databricks Model Serving endpoint used by OntoBricks Agents"* replaced.

3. **Empty-state messages** in both pickers: "No endpoints available" →
   **"No models configured — set ONTOBRICKS_LLM_MODELS"**. An empty list the user
   cannot act on is worse than no list.

4. **`domain/js/domain-information.js` — stale saved values.**
   `setSelectedLlmEndpoint` silently appended any saved value as a normal option,
   so a model no longer declared looked like a working choice and then failed at
   the provider. It is still shown — hiding it would silently rewrite the domain's
   setting on the next save — but labelled `— not in ONTOBRICKS_LLM_MODELS` in red.
   Also "-- Select an LLM endpoint --" → "-- Deployment default --", which is what
   an empty value now means.

5. **`partials/layout/help_modal.html`** — three places: the prerequisites list,
   the wizard walkthrough, and FAQ 6, which explained the old Databricks-only
   model. FAQ 6 now states the split explicitly: provider and credential are
   per-deployment, the domain picks a model.

6. **Help Center docs** — 13 edits across `user-guide.md`, `product.md`,
   `features.md`, `api.md`, `architecture.md`, `deployment.md`. Two were factually
   wrong rather than merely stale: `architecture.md`'s ResponsesAgent table still
   documented `custom_inputs.endpoint_name` (a contract I renamed to `model` last
   commit), and `product.md` still opened with *"OntoBricks runs as a Databricks
   App"*, which P7 deleted.

## Deliberately NOT added: base URL / API key fields

There is no UI to set `ONTOBRICKS_LLM_BASE_URL` or `ONTOBRICKS_LLM_API_KEY`, and
there should not be. Persisting an API key in the registry's settings table would
put a credential somewhere the UI reads and writes, where it would land in
`export_for_save`, in domain exports, and plausibly in a log line. Provider and
credential stay environment/secret-only; the UI chooses among models the operator
declared. The settings tab now says this in as many words, so the absence reads as
a decision rather than an omission.

## New guard

`TestLLMUICopy` in `tests/units/front/test_frontend_canonical_values.py` (5 tests):
no template or JS file claims the LLM must be a Databricks Model Serving endpoint;
the settings tab names the provider variables; both pickers name
`ONTOBRICKS_LLM_MODELS` in their empty state.

Verified by re-injecting the old copy — 1 failure — and reverting.

